{
  "abstract": "Can deletion tuning retain source capability after a finite development search when additive controls receive comparable selection? We compare strict binary gate deletion, arbitrary gate-only updates and full tuning of Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct on MMLU-Pro psychology. Each method receives three development parents trained for 360 measured seconds and four fixed deployment strengths; a common target/retention rule selects one setting before three fresh training seeds and reserved evaluation questions. The source panel uses ARC-Challenge and WinoGrande, whose final base accuracies exceed uniform-option references. Selected target accuracy rises from 11.00% to 28.00%, 29.33% and 26.83% for deletion, gate-only and full tuning. Equal-task source macro changes are +1.780, +1.585 and +3.023 percentage points; all pass the prespecified approximate three-method-adjusted noninferiority rule against a three-point loss. Deletion's target contrasts remain inconclusive, rather than establishing superiority or equivalence. Prespecified parent controls show that full-tuning shrinkage loses 8.500 target points while gaining 4.690 source macro points; deletion's selected support restriction loses 3.667 target points without a clear retention benefit. Deletion stores 3.757–3.865 MB of compressed incremental payload versus 245.588–245.982 MB for gate-only tuning, but uses more training allocated memory: 8.523 versus 6.803 GiB. Complete compressed base dependencies and 10,787.9 released GPU-lease seconds are accounted for. Lossless per-question predictions, frozen sources and portable replay support the results. One model, one target, two public source tasks, three seeds and a small search limit generalization; no deletion ceiling, internal mechanism or unique preservation advantage is established.",
